e strongest direct support for a viral transmission etiology over hereditary cancer syndromes?", "result_summary": "Recent familial viral exposure most strongly supports vesicular viral infection (B1) over hereditary cancer, but doesn't eliminate malignant or hereditary considerations.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_for", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the normal vitals and absence of systemic vasculitis signs (E6-E10) the best evidence for a benign vascular process rather than malignant neoplastic proliferation?", "result_summary": "Benign vasculitis signs (B2) gain moderate support from vital stability, while malignant vascular proliferation (B4) is weakly excluded.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2": "moderate_for", "B4": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of constitutional symptoms (E6, E8) weakly challenge the likelihood of a vesicular viral skin infection in the context of asymptomatic viral shedding?", "result_summary": "Mild attenuation of B1 plausibility due to absence of systemic symptoms, but asymptomatic shedding preserves viral considerations.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B4": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal temperature and lack of systemic features (E6, E10) challenge the plausibility of hereditary cancer syndrome as a primary cutaneous manifestation?", "result_summary": "Hereditary cancer syndrome (B3) weakly contradicted by stable vitals, which are more typical of acute infections than primary skin malignancy.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B3": "weak_against", "B5": "neutral"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.4251968503937008, "B2": 0.3543307086614173, "B3": 0.14173228346456693, "B4": 0.03937007874015748, "B5": 0.03937007874015748}, {"B1": 0.47976307996051337, "B2": 0.3998025666337612, "B3": 0.07107601184600199, "B4": 0.0246791707798618, "B5": 0.0246791707798618, "B1.1": 0.29985192497532087, "B1.2": 0.14992596248766044, "B1.3": 0.02998519249753209}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No evidence of airway compromise, severe respiratory issues, uncontrolled haemorrhage, or major haemodynamic instability. Vital signs are stable, and the clinical context points to investigation of viral infection vs benign vascular process as priority.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading viral hypothesis (B1, posterior 0.48) remains below 0.75 threshold. Dangerous hereditary cancer syndromes (B3, danger 0.9 but posterior 0.07) and inflammatory conditions (B1.2, danger 0.7) still require evaluation through unutilized discriminators like PCR testing."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.825, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3998025666337612, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 39-year-old man presents to his doctor for a wellness checkup. He is concerned about a rash that does not seem to be improving. He was recently exposed to his grandfather who has vesicular lesions on his skin and is being treated. He has a family history of skin cancer, colon cancer, and ovarian cancer. The patient has a medical history of asthma and seasonal allergies. His temperature is 98.6°F (37.0°C), blood pressure is 137/98 mmHg, pulse is 90/min, respirations are 14/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam reveals the finding in Figure A. Which of the following describes this patient's most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Benign capillary proliferation\nB. Edema of the epidermis\nC. Healthy dermatologic development\nD. Malignant blood vessel proliferation\nE. Viral infection", "static_question": "Which of the following describes this patient's most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Benign capillary proliferation"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Edema of the epidermis"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Healthy dermatologic development"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malignant blood vessel proliferation"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Viral infection"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Age: 39-year-old man", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: concerned about a rash not improving", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Recent exposure: grandfather with vesicular lesions being treated", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Family history: skin cancer, colon cancer, ovarian cancer", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Medical history: asthma and seasonal allergies", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Temperature: 98.6°F (37.0°C)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "direct", "content": "Blood pressure: 137/98 mmHg", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E8", "kind": "direct", "content": "Pulse: 90/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E9", "kind": "direct", "content": "Respirations: 14/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E10", "kind": "direct", "content": "Oxygen saturation: 98% on room air", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E11", "kind": "image_reference", "content": "Physical exam finding in Figure A — image not available in text form", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Multiple cutaneous malignancies', 'Dermatological manifestations of systemic disorders', 'Skin cancer'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=83%, source=both]\n\nHereditary Cancer Syndrome (Skin/Non-skin Related) vs Autoimmune/Connective Tissue Disease with Vesicular Manifestations:\n  Favours first:  abnormal fallopian tube morphology, breast carcinoma, neoplasm of the pancreas, ovarian neoplasm, prostate cancer\n  Favours second: abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, alopecia, arthralgia, arthritis, autoimmunity\n\nHereditary Cancer Syndrome (Skin/Non-skin Related) vs Vesicular Herpes Simplex Virus Infection (HSV-1/HSV-2):\n  Favours first:  abnormal fallopian tube morphology, breast carcinoma, neoplasm of the pancreas, ovarian neoplasm, prostate cancer\n  Favours second: body aches, burning sensation, eczema herpeticum, encephalitis, eye pain\n\nHereditary Cancer Syndrome (Skin/Non-skin Related) vs Varicella-Zoster Virus Infection (VZV) or Chickenpox/Shingles:\n  Favours first:  abnormal fallopian tube morphology, breast carcinoma, neoplasm of the pancreas, ovarian neoplasm, prostate cancer\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nHereditary Cancer Syndrome (Skin/Non-skin Related) vs Other Viral Etiologies (e.g., HHV-6/7, Coxsackievirus, Asymptomatic Shedding):\n  Favours first:  abnormal fallopian tube morphology, breast carcinoma, neoplasm of the pancreas, ovarian neoplasm, prostate cancer\n  Favours second: bluish skin, chest pain, chills, confusion, cough\n\nAutoimmune/Connective Tissue Disease with Vesicular Manifestations vs Vesicular Herpes Simplex Virus Infection (HSV-1/HSV-2):\n  Favours first:  abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, alopecia, arthralgia, arthritis, autoimmunity\n  Favours second: body aches, burning sensation, eczema herpeticum, encephalitis, eye pain\n\nAutoimmune/Connective Tissue Disease with Vesicular Manifestations vs Varicella-Zoster Virus Infection (VZV) or Chickenpox/Shingles:\n  Favours first:  abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, arthritis, autoimmunity, avascular necrosis, cardiac symptoms\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nAutoimmune/Connective Tissue Disease with Vesicular Manifestations vs Other Viral Etiologies (e.g., HHV-6/7, Coxsackievirus, Asymptomatic Shedding):\n  Favours first:  abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, alopecia, arthralgia, arthritis, autoimmunity\n  Favours second: bluish skin, chills, confusion, cough, headache\n\nVesicular Herpes Simplex Virus Infection (HSV-1/HSV-2) vs Varicella-Zoster Virus Infection (VZV) or Chickenpox/Shingles:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, eczema herpeticum, encephalitis, eye pain, fluid-filled blisters\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nVesicular Herpes Simplex Virus Infection (HSV-1/HSV-2) vs Other Viral Etiologies (e.g., HHV-6/7, Coxsackievirus, Asymptomatic Shedding):\n  Favours first:  body aches, burning sensation, eczema herpeticum, encephalitis, eye pain\n  Favours second: bluish skin, chest pain, chills, confusion, cough\n\nVaricella-Zoster Virus Infection (VZV) or Chickenpox/Shingles vs Other Viral Etiologies (e.g., HHV-6/7, Coxsackievirus, Asymptomatic Shedding):\n  Favours first:  abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n  Favours second: bluish skin, chest pain, chills, cough, muscle pain\n\nRelated to Hereditary Cancer Syndrome (Skin/Non-skin Related): autosomal dominant disease, breast-ovarian cancer, familial, susceptibility to, familial ovarian cancer\n\nRelated to Autoimmune/Connective Tissue Disease with Vesicular Manifestations: autoimmune disease of musculoskeletal system, autoimmune neuropathy, bone disease\n\nRelated to Vesicular Herpes Simplex Virus Infection (HSV-1/HSV-2): congenital varicella syndrome, fetal cytomegalovirus syndrome, herpes simplex infectious disease\n\nRelated to Varicella-Zoster Virus Infection (VZV) or Chickenpox/Shingles: chickenpox, congenital varicella syndrome, facial nerve palsy due to herpes zoster infection\n\n[RAG context for 'A 39-year-old man presents to his doctor for a wellness checkup' (score=0.83):]\n  Source: Undifferentiated connective tissue disease: state of the art on clinical practice guidelines. > Etiology\n  Researchers have tried to investigate the underlying cause of this condition and what can trigger these disease flares. T-regulatory cell populations were thought to be the culprit in starting such a \n[RAG context for 'A 39-year-old man presents to his doctor for a wellness checkup' (score=0.85):]"}
